---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Types of Pregnancy Discrimination | pregnancydiscriminationcalc.com';
const description = 'Overview of pregnancy discrimination types: termination, failure to accommodate, demotion, FMLA retaliation, and PWFA violations.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Types of Pregnancy Discrimination</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Termination</h2><p>Most common claim. Timing is critical evidence: terminated shortly after announcing pregnancy or requesting leave raises a strong inference of discrimination.</p>
  <h2>Failure to accommodate (PWFA)</h2><p>Under the PWFA, employers must provide reasonable accommodations (modified duties, more frequent breaks, temporary transfer) for pregnancy-related limitations absent undue hardship. Failure to engage in the interactive accommodation process is itself a violation.</p>
  <h2>Demotion or pay reduction</h2><p>Reassigning a pregnant employee to a lower-paying position &ldquo;while pregnant&rdquo; or denying promotion due to pregnancy. Requires showing non-pregnant similarly situated employees were treated more favorably.</p>
  <h2>FMLA and CFRA retaliation</h2><p>Retaliating against employees for taking maternity leave. This overlaps with FMLA retaliation and pregnancy discrimination claims. Often the strongest claim for termination occurring immediately after return from leave.</p>
  <h2>Hostile environment</h2><p>Pregnancy-based harassment severe or pervasive enough to alter working conditions: derogatory comments about pregnancy, exclusion from meetings, pressure to resign, or demeaning treatment by a supervisor after pregnancy announcement.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-pregnancy-discrimination-damages-work">how damages work</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
